WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:06.163
Picture the company that runs a café's card payments.

2
00:00:06.163 --> 00:00:09.364
Every hour, a report adds up what each shop took.

3
00:00:09.364 --> 00:00:10.997
It takes about 6 minutes.

4
00:00:11.200 --> 00:00:16.012
Then the owners want totals per kind of shop: cafés, bookshops, grocers.

5
00:00:16.012 --> 00:00:23.565
Only the shops table knows each shop's kind, so a teammate adds one line that matches each payment with its shop.

6
00:00:23.767 --> 00:00:26.133
The report now takes 25 minutes.

7
00:00:26.133 --> 00:00:29.313
And the shops table is tiny, just 500 rows.

8
00:00:29.313 --> 00:00:31.754
So where did nineteen minutes go?

9
00:00:31.754 --> 00:00:33.159
Hold that question.

10
00:00:33.400 --> 00:00:38.457
The report runs on Spark, a program that runs one job across many computers.

11
00:00:38.457 --> 00:00:44.178
Spark cuts the payments into slices called partitions, and a task works on each slice.

12
00:00:44.178 --> 00:00:47.372
Here, a million payments sit in four partitions.

13
00:00:47.567 --> 00:00:51.662
Matching the rows of two tables that share a key is called a join.

14
00:00:51.662 --> 00:00:52.965
And here's the catch.

15
00:00:52.965 --> 00:00:56.440
Spark can only match two rows that sit in the same task.

16
00:00:56.633 --> 00:01:03.278
Shop 42 has payments in all four partitions, and its own row is in the shops table, somewhere else.

17
00:01:03.278 --> 00:01:06.097
So before any matching, rows have to move.

18
00:01:06.300 --> 00:01:08.912
Picture a post office at the end of the day.

19
00:01:08.912 --> 00:01:12.415
Four clerks each hold a sack of letters from all over town.

20
00:01:12.600 --> 00:01:18.360
Every letter has a postcode, and a one page list says which district each postcode is in.

21
00:01:18.360 --> 00:01:21.531
The job is to write the district on every letter.

22
00:01:21.700 --> 00:01:22.585
Let's map it.

23
00:01:22.585 --> 00:01:24.286
The clerks are the tasks.

24
00:01:24.286 --> 00:01:26.259
The letters are the payments.

25
00:01:26.259 --> 00:01:28.437
The postcode is the shop number.

26
00:01:28.437 --> 00:01:31.227
And the one page list is the shops table.

27
00:01:31.433 --> 00:01:33.222
Way one moves everything.

28
00:01:33.222 --> 00:01:40.449
Each task works out a number for every row: hash the shop number, divide by four, keep the remainder.

29
00:01:40.449 --> 00:01:41.523
Shop 42 gets 3.

30
00:01:41.700 --> 00:01:49.557
Like clerks sorting letters into four pigeonholes, one per van, each task writes its rows to its own disk in four blocks.

31
00:01:50.100 --> 00:01:57.766
Then each task on the far side, called a reduce task, collects its block from every file, like a van doing its round.

32
00:01:57.766 --> 00:01:59.863
Spark calls this move a shuffle.

33
00:02:00.033 --> 00:02:03.959
The shops go through the same shuffle, with the same rule.

34
00:02:03.959 --> 00:02:08.495
So shop 42's row lands in reduce task 3, right beside its payments.

35
00:02:08.667 --> 00:02:16.251
Each reduce task sorts both piles by shop number and walks them together, like merging two sorted piles of letters.

36
00:02:16.251 --> 00:02:17.900
That's a sort-merge join.

37
00:02:17.900 --> 00:02:21.593
It works at any size, because sorting can spill to disk.

38
00:02:21.800 --> 00:02:23.429
But count what moved.

39
00:02:23.429 --> 00:02:30.334
Every payment and every shop: 1,000,500 rows, written to disk, read back and sent across.

40
00:02:30.334 --> 00:02:34.833
In the lesson's demo, that's four stages and about 9.4 MB.

41
00:02:35.067 --> 00:02:39.145
Back in the post office, that's a lot of driving for a one page list.

42
00:02:39.145 --> 00:02:41.864
Wouldn't it be nicer to move the list instead?

43
00:02:42.033 --> 00:02:43.093
So photocopy it.

44
00:02:43.093 --> 00:02:48.456
Every clerk gets a copy and writes the district on each letter right at the desk.

45
00:02:48.456 --> 00:02:50.707
How many letters have to move now?

46
00:02:50.707 --> 00:02:51.767
Pause and guess.

47
00:02:55.000 --> 00:02:55.405
None.

48
00:02:55.405 --> 00:02:57.915
Not one letter leaves the desk.

49
00:02:58.200 --> 00:02:59.584
Spark does the same.

50
00:02:59.584 --> 00:03:06.503
The driver, the program that plans the job, collects the 500 shops and sends a copy to every worker.

51
00:03:06.503 --> 00:03:08.993
Spark calls these workers executors.

52
00:03:09.167 --> 00:03:16.090
Each task builds a hash table from its copy: a lookup that finds a shop in one step, like a HashMap.

53
00:03:16.090 --> 00:03:21.489
Then it streams its 250,000 payments through it and joins them where they sit.

54
00:03:21.633 --> 00:03:23.919
That's a broadcast hash join.

55
00:03:23.919 --> 00:03:27.546
No payment is shuffled, and nothing is sorted.

56
00:03:27.546 --> 00:03:33.379
In the demo, it took 91 milliseconds, against 165 for the sort-merge join.

57
00:03:33.379 --> 00:03:34.247
About half.

58
00:03:34.433 --> 00:03:37.213
So why didn't Spark do this for our report?

59
00:03:37.213 --> 00:03:42.255
It does broadcast on its own, when its size guess for one side is under 10 MB.

60
00:03:42.467 --> 00:03:43.494
Here's the trap.

61
00:03:43.494 --> 00:03:46.705
The report keeps only the shops still in business.

62
00:03:46.705 --> 00:03:54.538
After a filter, Spark often can't tell how small a table has become, so it kept its guess for the whole table: over 10 MB.

63
00:03:54.538 --> 00:03:57.043
It weighed the shops before their diet.

64
00:03:57.233 --> 00:04:00.927
So it planned a sort-merge join, and every payment took the van.

65
00:04:00.927 --> 00:04:03.177
That's where the nineteen minutes went.

66
00:04:03.367 --> 00:04:05.746
When you know a side is small, say so.

67
00:04:05.746 --> 00:04:07.062
Wrap it in broadcast.

68
00:04:07.062 --> 00:04:09.880
A hint is a request, and Spark can refuse it.

69
00:04:09.880 --> 00:04:13.825
Here it copies the shops, and the payments stay where they are.

70
00:04:14.067 --> 00:04:16.655
Now, where the photocopy picture breaks.

71
00:04:16.655 --> 00:04:19.115
Copying one page costs almost nothing.

72
00:04:19.115 --> 00:04:25.457
But a broadcast table passes through the driver, and a full copy lands in every executor's memory.

73
00:04:25.633 --> 00:04:32.933
Broadcast a 2 GB table to 100 executors and you've moved 200 GB, if the driver survives collecting it.

74
00:04:32.933 --> 00:04:36.583
That's photocopying the phone book for every clerk.

75
00:04:36.583 --> 00:04:39.947
Keep broadcasts to tables of tens of megabytes.

76
00:04:40.133 --> 00:04:41.961
So the moral of the story.

77
00:04:41.961 --> 00:04:45.125
Both sides big: shuffle both, and sort-merge.

78
00:04:45.125 --> 00:04:49.625
One side small: send it to the data, and the big side stays put.

79
00:04:49.900 --> 00:04:51.076
One quick check.

80
00:04:51.076 --> 00:04:55.414
A report joins the payments with a customers table of 3 GB.

81
00:04:55.414 --> 00:04:57.988
Should you broadcast the customers?

82
00:04:57.988 --> 00:04:59.164
Pause and think.

83
00:05:02.400 --> 00:05:02.623
No.

84
00:05:02.623 --> 00:05:06.934
Three gigabytes in every executor is the phone book again.

85
00:05:06.934 --> 00:05:09.609
Both sides are big, so shuffle both.
